WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.714
Type III covers low-pressure chillers. These are commercial systems — big
equipment, big consequences.

00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:51.429
Water is the enemy of low-pressure systems. Many chillers have water-cooled
condensers and water-chilled evaporators with water flowing through tubes inside
the refrigerant vessel. A tube leak allows water into the refrigerant circuit

00:00:51.429 --> 00:01:17.143
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:42.857
Hydrolysis: Water reacting with refrigerant to form hydrochloric or hydrofluoric
acid.

00:01:42.857 --> 00:02:08.571
Here's why this matters on the job: Regular oil analysis on chillers catches
water contamination before it destroys the compressor.

00:02:08.571 --> 00:02:34.286
Watch out for these: Water in the system forms acid. And — Oil analysis detects
contamination early

00:02:34.286 --> 00:03:00.000
Why does a low-pressure system have air leaking in rather than refrigerant
leaking out? Think about the pressure relationship.

